---------------------------------------------\nMathematics – Ramis Movassagh (https://ramismovassagh.wordpress.com/category/mathematics/)\nciteturn6search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Chatterjee et al’s result is equivalent to the averaging process on the complete graph $K_n$ with respect to the $L^1$-norm. ... $K_n$ is the complete graph (i.e., the clique), and $S_n$ is the star graph where we plot $T(n)$ for two different initializations.\n\n# Category: Mathematics\n\n## Repeated Averages on Graphs\n\nWith Mario Szegedy and Guanyang Wang, we published this paper (arXiv). It is purely a classical probability theory question whose original motivation comes partly from quantum circuits and partly from kinetics in statistical physics. This blog is meant to convey the intuition and complement the paper by providing material that couldn’t be embedded in a paper (e.g., videos).\n\nThe problem: Suppose you have a vector of real numbers and at every discrete time, you pick a random pair of entries and replace their values by their average. How fast does the vector converge to the constant stationary vector? I discussed this problem with Persi Diaconis at a conference that took place at MIT in Aug. of 2019. Persi got interested and told me that apparently Jean Bourgain had asked him the same question in the 1980s. Subsequently Sourav Chatterjee, Persi Diaconis, Allan Sly and Liangfu Zhang obtained this result for this process. In v2 of their paper, they proved a cutoff phenomenon too.\n\nThe vector of real numbers in the problem above can be thought of as the values of the nodes of a complete graph (fully connected). The averaging process is that at every discrete time you pick a random edge $(i,j)$ and replace the value of the nodes incident to it with their average: $v_i, v_j\\rightarrow \\frac{v_i+v_j}{2}$ . It is a basic fact of probability theory that the final (stationary) distribution must have the same value, i.e., .\n\n$v(\\infty) = \\frac{1}{n}(1,1,\\dots,1)^T  $\n\nwhere without loss of generality we took $v$ to be a vector of probabilities.\n\nSo the question is how fast and with respect to what metric is the final distribution reached? For example, how long does it take to get $\\epsilon-$close to the stationary distribution in an $L^1$-norm or in $L^2 $-norm?\n\nChatterjee et al’s result is equivalent to the averaging process on the complete graph $K_n$ with respect to the $L^1$-norm. Our paper is a generalization to arbitrary connected graphs and with respect to various norms. The $L^1$-norm between the state vector at time $v(t)$ and the final state is:\n\n$T(t) = \\left\\Vert v(t) - \\frac{1}{n}(1,1,\\dots,1)^T \\right\\Vert_1= \\sum^n_{i=1} \\lvert v_i(t) - \\frac{1}{n} \\rvert $\n\nBelow is the plot of convergence of $T(t) $ for three different graphs:\n\nImage\n\nFig. Expected $L^1$ convergence as a function of time (averaged over $20 $ runs in each of the three cases) of the Averaging Process for three graphs with $n=100,000,000$ nodes. $K_n$ is the complete graph (i.e., the clique), and $S_n$ is the star graph where we plot $T(n)$ for two different initializations.\n\nI encourage you to look at the Figures 5-8 in the paper as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://owu.terracat.net/search/?__owu_origin_v1=aHR0cHM6Ly9hcnhpdi5vcmc&query=Bhattacharya%2C+B+B&searchtype=author)\nciteturn6search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Moreover, this condition is necessary if and only if $H$ is a star-graph.\nMore generally, for a general connected graph $H$, let $T(H, G_n)$ be the number of monochromatic copies of $H$ in a uniformly random coloring of the vertices of the graph $G_n$ with $c_n$ colors. In this paper we show that $T(H, G_n)$ converges to $\\mathrm{Pois}(λ)$ whenever $\\mathbb E T(H, G_n) \\rightarrow λ$ and $\\mathrm{Var} T(H, G_n) \\rightarrow λ$, that is, the asymptotic Poisson distribution of $T(H, G_n)